choc-webview2-navigation.patch: the WebView2 backend asks the app before it
navigates, opens a window, or delivers a page message (B446, ADR-194 D-S5).

Base:     choc a08bfd84494921700703a8433b9d1a304edfafd2 (the libs/choc pin)
Applied:  at configure time, to a build-tree copy of libs/choc/choc, by
          libs/patches/apply_patch.cmake. The submodule is never edited.
Checked:  tools/choc_patch_check.py (./verify fast).

PURPOSE. choc keeps its ICoreWebView2 object private, so an app cannot
register NavigationStarting, FrameNavigationStarting or NewWindowRequested,
or read the source of a page message. This adds one option,
WebView::Options::allowNavigation (type, uri), which the WebView2 backend
consults from those three events and from WebMessageReceived. horde's
Windows backend (src/gui/hypersaw_gui_win.cpp) uses it to admit only its
embedded page, the rule the macOS backend applies in its own wrapper.

DEFAULT UNCHANGED. The patch only adds lines. With the option left empty no
new handler is registered, and the message path runs exactly as upstream.

UPSTREAM STATUS. Not yet proposed. choc takes feature requests as issues,
not pull requests (its CONTRIBUTING.md). A request is drafted for the
human's review; filing it outside the fleet takes the human's explicit yes.

REMOVAL CONDITION. Delete this patch and its wiring (libs/patches/README.md
lists every piece) when the pinned choc provides an equivalent hook. If the
upstream shape differs, port src/gui/hypersaw_gui_win.cpp to it in the same
change. Any bump of libs/choc turns tools/choc_patch_check.py red until the
Base line above is updated, so the question is asked at every bump.

--- a/choc/gui/choc_WebView.h
+++ b/choc/gui/choc_WebView.h
@@ -137,6 +137,23 @@
         /// otherwise wouldn't work by default. This lets you turn that off if you
         /// need to.
         bool enableDefaultClipboardKeyShortcutsInSafari = true;
+
+        /// The kinds of request that allowNavigation is asked about.
+        enum class NavigationType
+        {
+            page,       ///< a navigation of the top-level document
+            frame,      ///< a navigation inside a frame
+            newWindow,  ///< a request to open a new window
+            message     ///< a message to a bound function; the URI is the sending document's
+        };
+
+        /// Optional. If set, this is called on the message thread with the URI of
+        /// each request of the kinds above, and the request goes ahead only if it
+        /// returns true. A refused navigation is cancelled, a refused new window
+        /// is not opened, and a refused message is dropped (its call never
+        /// resolves). Leave it empty for the default behaviour, which allows
+        /// everything. Currently implemented by the WebView2 (Windows) backend.
+        std::function<bool(NavigationType, const std::string& uri)> allowNavigation;
     };
 
